# Labs 0.9.0 spike audit (read-only, 2026-10-22)

**Finding.** The saved 512-label candidate tail is a real *recorded elapsed-time regime* within one fresh-process block, not a percentile arithmetic error or one untrimmed outlier. Its cause is not identifiable from the saved Labs result. Labs' short conditional warmup, fresh process per block, forced collection around samples, and wall-clock timing make cold/JIT/GC or process scheduling plausible mechanisms across repositories. None is established as the cause of this block. Do not use this pooled p95 alone as a product admission gate or discard it as machine load.

## What the installed runner actually does

- The exact local package is `@pmndrs/labs` 0.9.0; `benches/labs.config.ts:9-17` requests eight blocks, a 0.5 s block budget, 20 minimum samples, and a selectable sampling policy. The local patch only threads `samplingPolicy` through CLI/config and maps `single-call` to `batch:false` (`patches/@pmndrs__labs@0.9.0.patch:1-19,34-73`; installed `dist/worker.mjs:8-18`). It does not change the timer, warmup, GC, trimming, or merge code. The candidate manifest says `single-call`, eight blocks, full suite (`matrix-candidate/manifest.json`).
- The dense 512/1024 benchmarks are generator (`kind:"yield"`) fixtures, but their yielded zero-argument callbacks run through `benchFn`, not the iterator timing path (`dist/src-DNyOXr2d.mjs:188-231`). Their callbacks include all setters and publication; 512 also traverses the scene and inspects draws (`benches/labs/package/batching.bench.ts:103-122`), while 1024 traverses the scene and checks publication (`benches/labs/package/stress.bench.ts:123-141`).
- Each block imports the benchmark file and measures the chosen trial in a **new child Node process** (`dist/worker.mjs:54-85`). The pilot fixes sample count and batching for later blocks; blocks run round-robin across benchmark identities (`dist/worker.mjs:160-185`; `dist/src-DNyOXr2d.mjs:700-714`). The candidate 512 and 1024 plans both have `batch:false`, 20 raw samples per block (`matrix-candidate/candidate.json`, the named runs' `stats.plan`).
- `benchFn` invokes the callback once before timing, then performs two more warmup calls **only if the first call takes at most 500,000 ns** (`dist/src-DNyOXr2d.mjs:241-278`). A 10–20 ms first call therefore normally gets just one untimed call in each fresh process. The comment at `:123` says the opposite of the condition; the code is authoritative. This is a reproducibility risk, not evidence that warmup caused the 113 ms block.
- On Node the Labs clock is `performance.now() * 1e6`, and a single-call sample is `t1 - t0` around the callback and result consumption (`dist/src-DNyOXr2d.mjs:63-84,389-423`). This measures elapsed wall time; suspension/preemption and runtime work within that interval are indistinguishable in the saved number. Labs forces GC before the generated sample loop and, by default, immediately before and after each timed sample (`dist/src-DNyOXr2d.mjs:332,380-384,411-423`; `:721-734,803-807`). The per-sample GC cost is added to the stopping budget and reported separately, not added to `samples[_]`. The callback's own GC, if any, remains inside its wall duration.
- Each 20-sample block sorts its samples and drops its two minima and two maxima; the merger pools the 16 survivors from each block and sorts them again (`dist/src-DNyOXr2d.mjs:427-445`; `dist/worker.mjs:111-140`). It preserves ordered block medians, calibration rates, and spreads, but loses sample chronology and block membership in the pooled array. CLI's `trimStats` omits the generated `debug` loop from the saved JSON (`dist/cli/cli.mjs:193-207`). The saved block summary cannot tell which exact tail sample was in block 0 or when within that block it occurred.
- The so-called clock calibration is a short software no-op throughput measurement (`dist/worker.mjs:30-38`; `dist/src-DNyOXr2d.mjs:973-1002`), not a hardware frequency readout. The 67.6% candidate “clock” drift comes from file-level no-op estimates (`dist/cli/cli.mjs:836-847`). Block rates are used for diagnostic normalization/veto of comparison verdicts (`dist/cli/cli.mjs:455-480`), while `mergeStats` saves raw elapsed samples unchanged. The noisy flag means median within-block robust spread exceeds 10%, not that a product defect or host load has been identified (`dist/cli/cli.mjs:796-819`; `dist/stats-DyCSLnVh.mjs:200-223`).

## What this result establishes

The saved candidate 512 run has 128 retained samples. Its eight block medians are **113.293, 11.488, 10.573, 12.660, 9.508, 9.234, 9.447, 9.191 ms**. Thirteen pooled retained samples exceed 50 ms; eleven exceed 100 ms. Thus the 117.499 ms pooled p95 reflects a sustained high block, even after per-block trimming. The 1024 candidate block medians are all 18.376–19.977 ms. The same-machine main 512 medians are 7.467–8.160 ms and published 512 medians are 7.989–10.557 ms (`matrix-candidate/candidate.json`, `matrix-main-final/baseline.json`, `matrix-published-final/baseline.json`, named run `stats.blocks.medians`). Candidate 512's other seven blocks remain slower than main but are nowhere near the 113 ms regime; the spike and the ordinary median deficit are separate observations.

Sorted retained sample values, nearest-rank p50 / p90 / p95 in ms (the arm files above are the source):

| Identity | Candidate | Main | Published 0.1.0 |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Rewrite 512 | 10.295 / 77.719 / 117.499 | 7.602 / 8.625 / 8.926 | 8.143 / 9.586 / 12.271 |
| Publish 1024 | 19.548 / 21.883 / 22.203 | 16.547 / 17.895 / 18.208 | 16.686 / 18.097 / 18.422 |

The saved result has no untrimmed chronological sample stream, per-call CPU time, context-switch counts, GC event alignment, optimization events, or process scheduling trace. Existing host load snapshots lack a per-sample time join. The browser 20-warmup probe has a different fixture/runtime and cannot prove that extra warmup fixes this Node block. No installed-source arithmetic defect was found that converts a ~10 ms callback into a ~113 ms sample. The most defensible common explanation today is **measurement exposure**: each fresh process gets little warmup, is forcibly collected around samples, and reports elapsed time that includes scheduling and runtime transitions. Which exposed mechanism produced this particular block remains open.

## Smallest conditional diagnostic

Run **one diagnostic-only reproduction on the exact existing 512 callback and exact candidate artifact**, with no product patch or new semantic fixture. Pin the installed Labs 0.9.0 files and local patch by hash. Use the same fresh-child, pilot/replay, single-call, eight-block lifecycle and 20 raw samples. Add a trace-only copy of Labs that records, before the existing sort/trim, block index, sample index, raw `t0/t1/diff`, warmup count, and whether each raw sample survives the unchanged trim. Wrap the existing yielded callback to record each invocation's `performance.now()` wall interval, `process.cpuUsage()` delta, `process.resourceUsage()` voluntary/involuntary context-switch deltas, and call index in a separate chronological ledger. Record process ID and monotonic start time; align the wrapper and Labs samples by call index within each child. Keep the output outside accepted benchmark results. Verify the trace-only diff changes no operation, sample arithmetic, stopping rule, or result returned to Labs. The wrapper and counters perturb the workload, so any outcome is diagnostic, not an admission timing.

Predeclare two arms and alternate their child order by pair: stock one untimed callback versus **20 additional untimed invocations** before the same sample loop (21 total, preserving the toggle parity of the original fixture). Implement the warmup override in the trace-only Labs copy after the normal first call; changing `warmup_samples` alone would have no effect on this slow callback because of the 500,000 ns threshold. Keep GC policy and timed callback identical. If the spike does not recur, the diagnostic is inconclusive; do not substitute its favorable p95 for the saved matrix. If it recurs, compare each high Labs diff with the corresponding callback ledger: high in Labs only points to timed wrapper/consume/clock effects; high wall with low process CPU plus context switches points to suspension; high wall and CPU with aligned GC/optimization events points to runtime or actual callback work. GC/optimization attribution would need event timestamps from `PerformanceObserver` or a separately identified V8 trace, because the saved `stats.gc` aggregate cannot align events to samples. A warmup-arm difference supports a warmup mechanism only when the same high regime recurs in stock blocks in the paired sitting; absence alone is not proof. Preserve original p50/p90 and block medians alongside every raw tail and report any instrumentation effect.
